      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "contents": [
            "localizedData",
            "nonLocalizedData"
          ],
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/google/osv-scanner",
          "isComprehensive": false,
          "language": "en-US",
          "locations": [],
          "name": "osv-scanner",
          "notifications": [],
          "rules": [
            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2019-10906",
                "PYSEC-2019-217",
                "GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "In Pallets Jinja before 2.10.1, `str.format_map` allows a sandbox escape.\n\nThe sandbox is used to restrict what code can be evaluated when rendering untrusted, user-provided templates. Due to the way string formatting works in Python, the `str.format_map` method could be used to escape the sandbox.\n\nThis issue was previously addressed for the `str.format` method in Jinja 2.8.1, which discusses the issue in detail. However, the less-common `str.format_map` method was overlooked. This release applies the same sandboxing to both methods.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade Jinja, you can override the `is_safe_attribute` method on the sandbox and explicitly disallow the `format_map` method on string objects.",
                "text": "In Pallets Jinja before 2.10.1, `str.format_map` allows a sandbox escape.\n\nThe sandbox is used to restrict what code can be evaluated when rendering untrusted, user-provided templates. Due to the way string formatting works in Python, the `str.format_map` method could be used to escape the sandbox.\n\nThis issue was previously addressed for the `str.format` method in Jinja 2.8.1, which discusses the issue in detail. However, the less-common `str.format_map` method was overlooked. This release applies the same sandboxing to both methods.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade Jinja, you can override the `is_safe_attribute` method on the sandbox and explicitly disallow the `format_map` method on string objects."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2019-10906](https://osv.dev/CVE-2019-10906)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2019-217](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2019-217), [GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2019-217](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2019-217)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e In Pallets Jinja before 2.10.1, str.format_map allows a sandbox escape.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e In Pallets Jinja before 2.10.1, `str.format_map` allows a sandbox escape.\n\u003e \n\u003e The sandbox is used to restrict what code can be evaluated when rendering untrusted, user-provided templates. Due to the way string formatting works in Python, the `str.format_map` method could be used to escape the sandbox.\n\u003e \n\u003e This issue was previously addressed for the `str.format` method in Jinja 2.8.1, which discusses the issue in detail. However, the less-common `str.format_map` method was overlooked. This release applies the same sandboxing to both methods.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade Jinja, you can override the `is_safe_attribute` method on the sandbox and explicitly disallow the `format_map` method on string objects.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/requirements.txt | jinja2 | 2.10 |\n| unknown:/src/requirements.txt | jinja2 | 2.10.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45 | jinja2 | 2.10.1 |\n| PYSEC-2019-217 | jinja2 | 2.10.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2019-10906\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2019-10906](https://osv.dev/CVE-2019-10906)**\n(Also published as: [PYSEC-2019-217](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2019-217), [GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45), ).\n\n## [PYSEC-2019-217](https://osv.dev/PYSEC-2019-217)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e In Pallets Jinja before 2.10.1, str.format_map allows a sandbox escape.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n## [GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45](https://osv.dev/GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e In Pallets Jinja before 2.10.1, `str.format_map` allows a sandbox escape.\n\u003e \n\u003e The sandbox is used to restrict what code can be evaluated when rendering untrusted, user-provided templates. Due to the way string formatting works in Python, the `str.format_map` method could be used to escape the sandbox.\n\u003e \n\u003e This issue was previously addressed for the `str.format` method in Jinja 2.8.1, which discusses the issue in detail. However, the less-common `str.format_map` method was overlooked. This release applies the same sandboxing to both methods.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade Jinja, you can override the `is_safe_attribute` method on the sandbox and explicitly disallow the `format_map` method on string objects.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/requirements.txt | jinja2 | 2.10 |\n| unknown:/src/requirements.txt | jinja2 | 2.10.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-462w-v97r-4m45 | jinja2 | 2.10.1 |\n| PYSEC-2019-217 | jinja2 | 2.10.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2019-10906\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
